ext} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347019} {:answer/id #uuid "d48896a5-f655-5550-962f-afc68a851fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910cdafc-67fc-587c-87f0-bdba48190064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346616} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b44f15-bb40-5519-8a0c-c8353afc8e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347910}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0f13e6-ba4b-56eb-b269-f07bb6ae2732" :question/math-academy-id "q-80303" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a7b19b50822235a2dcbfcdf74984735a1e26b2b775180b24bdc2bcbf24c588b.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x=-2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\nII. $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n\nIII. $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-2$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-2$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129006 :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/ce/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/59/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707 :topic/math-academy-id 1929 :topic/title "Introduction to Partial Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044 :topic/math-academy-id 152 :topic/title "The Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e1daaa-dc7f-57a9-81b8-22fbcb301a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321201} {:answer/id #uuid "81eb5789-543d-50e7-ad83-a4bfccc26cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322171} {:answer/id #uuid "948f60f4-5a9b-5003-8084-be16c06fae10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322328} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0190ae-90dd-5b97-b446-a58698208c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322370} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c111aa-65f0-573e-955f-760529a5afe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c111aa-65f0-573e-955f-760529a5afe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322531} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ab9650-e7f9-53e4-be63-104407f7ffee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323620}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0beb6dad-ec13-5ce0-a209-c7b3cfc3efa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38078" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e3546fa-15a2-5d61-96f6-97559389e16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u - v}{2u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127286} {:answer/id #uuid "afa8e959-c8de-5e85-aad5-1bf66bc06c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u + v}{2u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127287} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a56a0a-029e-5b5a-9aa1-2c4c31c0afe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2v - u}{2u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127288} {:answer/id #uuid "b78db8b2-468f-5a35-935d-4b2d118c364f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127289} {:answer/id #uuid "f889ffb2-5bf9-552c-b436-d9bad4eb9156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2u - v}{2u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127290}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce1f8a3-a444-5060-b21b-88d7f97c6039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127285}] :question/id #uuid "11d6f504-95b5-5c56-b8f4-c98216f1e26b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38244" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\ln (2u + v) \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543c2b9c-325b-52f0-b396-90565c46c411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276224} {:answer/id #uuid "92c19161-8314-50f9-8577-fa4d8021b843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276806} {:answer/id #uuid "d2669ec8-53ac-5fd9-8ed2-7a7d577e4da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277334} {:answer/id #uuid "f5091b71-1261-5b43-99a4-254b54528a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277623} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c8bae6-1f2b-5a5c-b655-a34f3416c74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3u-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2669ec8-53ac-5fd9-8ed2-7a7d577e4da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277334} :answer-field/id #uuid "810cd0e6-84ff-58ed-b739-3c8c64751fe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "680f7a12-0c16-5883-aa06-5376f25863fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160381" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4a3210-10ef-5b25-901e-ab593d9722a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275737} {:answer/id #uuid "97420ee7-fdf5-5150-958a-35de289cfc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276836} {:answer/id #uuid "e66d3a94-2dd5-54c7-b4bb-4f280703014c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277489} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4e4f14-3265-58a7-acfe-48a134dd8de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277697} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6c8ca7-ffb6-520e-b5af-e3963f09a153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4e4f14-3265-58a7-acfe-48a134dd8de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277697} :answer-field/id #uuid "beae5e95-9df5-5eed-ba44-7362dc1d3cb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278063}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c12a73a-3560-5ca0-8c93-5fcc98faad4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-160403" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de50ced-13d2-5b52-b330-c08b4accea21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276167} {:answer/id #uuid "7915ec34-4814-56e1-8467-fefea7d9c01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276575} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaa62e7-62ed-563f-ad65-988b473507fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277288} {:answer/id #uuid "e6764292-1e5d-59b1-9254-fa0803749a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277490} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ea9dc-6100-51cd-aced-9070d203b9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ea9dc-6100-51cd-aced-9070d203b9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277665} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b727bf-5aef-5fca-a0c0-b0c3271d7bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277825}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18d1f220-8bd2-586b-90ca-87751084600f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160405" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062ad924-b9ac-5458-b29b-3d01c466d8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348501} {:answer/id #uuid "0d89f367-4443-5fe8-8644-145ec0dab50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348565} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2f2b26-f487-53ac-904f-278604ef6f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349000} {:answer/id #uuid "96d294c8-f380-5d82-a452-5c76194ff827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349799} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9c4eab-46e0-5668-a2bc-a3d8dd51f5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v){sin}^{2}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d89f367-4443-5fe8-8644-145ec0dab50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348565} :answer-field/id #uuid "03915e0d-dd82-5c3f-80f0-7fc6e5e74eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350711}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a033a41-4f6f-52b1-9278-922a7269908f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81252" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\, & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0a2d56-1032-5653-bdf7-fcbbe224983e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383503} {:answer/id #uuid "9adbf7bb-44c3-5433-afaf-0547a0ee87dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383504} {:answer/id #uuid "39011aac-b9c0-5a20-9843-852f2d31526e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383505} {:answer/id #uuid "bab3f78e-3f56-585e-b9ce-112a1f07828c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383506} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf863f-082a-5c11-b7dd-3625aad8b760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c0a2d56-1032-5653-bdf7-fcbbe224983e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383503} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd5cc0a-1c3d-5de1-81cf-3a2da295967f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383502}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d4d00b9-f07a-56e5-8c62-1dc781808a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160404" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u-5v \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u-5v$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-5 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{u+v} & \\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u+v})-(-5)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{u+v}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb38347-2e82-5d17-ad40-6a0e81c54f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383510} {:answer/id #uuid "838ab111-2c3c-527c-8408-c8c361dfef0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383511} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4c3726-e2bb-58c5-a4ce-829f4ad3a100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}(uv)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383512} {:answer/id #uuid "a7595bc5-8856-5a45-a98a-43e423b6db07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(v-u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383513} {:answer/id #uuid "85fd7556-d652-5f2d-a0d4-cf974f4ae8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7595bc5-8856-5a45-a98a-43e423b6db07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{u+v}(v-u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383513} :answer-field/id #uuid "b727cda1-e9ad-5bca-9a2b-b11956b21803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383509}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd9f0081-13ed-51ab-acfe-3bfbc0b818a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-160406" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x={e}^{u+v}$ and $y={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }2u\\, & \\,\\text{ }2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03936218-a08a-5085-89bd-b21159a8a2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385410} {:answer/id #uuid "238d09ca-3e08-5ff2-b3e4-4a29a753d669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385411} {:answer/id #uuid "71ccc347-7069-5697-97bd-d00b2c229b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385412} {:answer/id #uuid "972dd51b-f2ed-5cc0-a383-aec169b7a34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385413} {:answer/id #uuid "ede2af95-8e48-5ae2-8728-186263d78888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡v}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03936218-a08a-5085-89bd-b21159a8a2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385410} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c8b93f-fcc0-528b-abf0-be921590f6fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385409}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b88d393d-4f24-5726-a4c5-591ca47c0041" :question/math-academy-id "q-38241" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(u/2)} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}) \\\\ \\,0\\, & \\,\\text{ }\\text{ }\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564d868a-9fde-5394-a0a7-ee34c0c4d724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385757} {:answer/id #uuid "a85952d3-86d1-5ecc-ad78-7dcdecf1e567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff785874-5a5d-5b25-b151-f49876d09835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385759} {:answer/id #uuid "832628ab-7157-5368-b5e4-55126e572f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385760} {:answer/id #uuid "e3966b05-dcec-5f9b-b1c6-e7031cf3e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff785874-5a5d-5b25-b151-f49876d09835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385759} :answer-field/id #uuid "38071234-9bc0-5b00-b590-cccd8d7c31d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385756}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "defe61a0-21a4-5ecd-abfb-89db5317a74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81264" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 2v-(-2v)\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =4uv+4uv \\\\  & =8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129015 :question/id #uuid "9de92581-00d5-5343-90be-3b5527bfab49" :question/math-academy-id "e-7283" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu =3x-2y, \\quad v=x+y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(3x-2y)^2 - y - x = 0$ and the line $x+y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/87/87b44aa661a51ee39011c33c2eb000edcebb127508d45d5819339cf532258f3e.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu = 3x-2y, \\qquad v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/88/88c92ebc206481c3ef86661ee413358bb060cb85f642148c609de0bf1a9da2d1.png)\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $3x-2y = u$ and $x+y = v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-2y)^{2}-y-x=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y = v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2, 4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\, u^2 \\leq v \\leq 4\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a3d0211dfbe18e5737b62e8b61cf9308af2735ef827d14bae7b5dbfffaa4faa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = (5)^{-1} = \\dfrac 15.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da85f713-e8f7-5e04-b9e6-b1b33383be9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049032 :topic/math-academy-id 4134 :topic/title "Transformations of Regions Between Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/math-academy-id 2151 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9315f9a2-e02a-52a0-9b61-22998fdd4b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127340} {:answer/id #uuid "a2493839-f867-5e6b-86ab-21e93542af83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127341} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf23205-9937-580f-9487-67df5e047e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127342} {:answer/id #uuid "fbba71af-ed9d-5b0d-9f28-8c1f36bd3fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127343} {:answer/id #uuid "176c442b-d740-5c8e-bae2-04e05f97f2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127344}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3868bc82-87b4-5f23-bdbc-a27b10c54df7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127339}] :question/id #uuid "d5cbb250-8e64-5b66-b88a-3ef280b7ad9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38374" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = x - y, v = y$,\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2} - y = 0$ and the line $y = 1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27118a3-c76e-5b3d-9e7c-f5ee73b3dd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128750} {:answer/id #uuid "d4850885-2218-5bce-81f2-c97c8186a79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128751} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ced5e5-97d0-5199-8d59-eac634f2316b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128752} {:answer/id #uuid "2e28eb35-e5a7-5365-a4b9-86ee2f536567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128753} {:answer/id #uuid "65bc1ad3-22b8-57c4-8712-d40b0054dfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128754}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8d5aa2fb-b182-54dc-8577-af2e5c597314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128749} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf7e1f1-fcf9-543c-97ee-b919265bfc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373306} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bf893e-8435-5b7e-b5c4-19722cff781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373307} {:answer/id #uuid "4deced36-0d95-50f5-af94-23b1383c8b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373308} {:answer/id #uuid "3395c3cb-8544-536c-a34a-982908787158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373309} {:answer/id #uuid "55a5789a-60d9-53af-abe8-31a45948a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5bf893e-8435-5b7e-b5c4-19722cff781d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373307} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50d9972-aaca-5f8a-98d1-1ba9d85277f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373305}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5371e22a-fae1-5a1c-87b6-2b32ed156fc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-82874" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0$ and  the line $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x-y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+y=u \\\\ x-y=v\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{u-v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{u-v}{2}=0\\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{4} \\\\ v=u\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}(u-1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le u}.\n$$\n\n![](images/1e/1ed905e319e689d3fc7955a30f58e0f3ae2e7a751c27b80201d2b43f58b4206c.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a307a4-5e3d-5e26-9870-e4654bb643c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371908} {:answer/id #uuid "9175a99b-56d0-595b-86db-511244542711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371909} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae05fa3-0d0d-5167-94c2-8ea479a334e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371910} {:answer/id #uuid "222f9a03-a010-5d70-a31b-68c15b8ab921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371911} {:answer/id #uuid "86cdb592-c0ee-5680-989f-fa4c9b6fb697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9175a99b-56d0-595b-86db-511244542711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371909} :answer-field/id #uuid "579f73d3-5ee3-5e3b-9cb0-45b6635a75cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f411b679-c6ae-56bb-9f50-ac0896593964" :question/math-academy-id "q-160885" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines $y+2x=1,$ $y-2x=1$ and the curve ${y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+2x=u$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x=1\\,⟹\\,u=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y-2x=v$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=y+2x$ and $v=y-2x$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,(y+2x)(y-2x)=4,\\,⟹\\,uv=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}uv=4 \\\\ u=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=1 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}uv=4 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=4 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,4)$ and $(4,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{4}{u}}.\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30fff9564451baadff3cae48dbfb91dc08fc8591e596e7cd2f65d8cfc6c9c5a6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(4)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{4/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[v]_{1}^{4/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e834b2d-26ec-5a0b-b024-653762ca7f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373313} {:answer/id #uuid "8232f0ab-a3f1-5a06-a6cb-8418ed7a1099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373314} {:answer/id #uuid "36c18b51-d2d9-5561-b308-2fdc610bb877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373315} {:answer/id #uuid "88c8bbb9-1718-53bf-8d76-f55389c1603f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373316} {:answer/id #uuid "29ff210b-32a3-59a3-9402-2b61a8d6a02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8232f0ab-a3f1-5a06-a6cb-8418ed7a1099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373314} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e5a6329-2104-5c1b-84f2-ccc6d5d0b453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db42860a-5849-59fe-9226-d7cef72a335f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82878" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x-y)^{2}+y=2$ and the line $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v+1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,(y+1)=3-(x-y)^{2}\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}.}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9cec7ff29557aa14cd19309e9180482a44091e7ef215205285822674e7bc25b0.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3-{u}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e39adcf-f9fe-5dd8-8121-889b47f3e44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373334} {:answer/id #uuid "eddeac90-01c0-5833-a667-85aceff8ed1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373335} {:answer/id #uuid "50017198-4384-5924-9339-b114ae17f28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373336} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d1083-137d-51cd-af77-24049d080386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373337} {:answer/id #uuid "19278225-d301-581a-a354-aa480305af42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b3d1083-137d-51cd-af77-24049d080386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373337} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0492151-0ea1-564c-95ed-c1870ce912b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373333}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "851a883c-83e2-57d3-b805-41682b65a2ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-83943" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $y-(x-2y)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(x-2y)^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}+2 \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}+2\\le v\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca755cd11609e7c704adb37eb867ff8cc48bc5fc44b29cd777269be5791d9160.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-({u}^{2}+2))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76d7d05-c899-566f-a267-b260f4f22494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373341} {:answer/id #uuid "e610ef2b-0037-56d0-bead-5ff13607271a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373342} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3876d-8c80-5bc3-9185-9669f13f9f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373343} {:answer/id #uuid "639ae9f3-3123-5bb0-aa7c-f883873eebe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373344} {:answer/id #uuid "8823d84a-4e7a-5110-b70c-63b3e1900363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3876d-8c80-5bc3-9185-9669f13f9f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373343} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3452f16-065c-5d3f-b342-bf97b568f3a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373340}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "672b45e6-0aa1-59c9-9e64-dd97d59af187" :question/math-academy-id "q-83947" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(y+3x)^{2}-x=3$ and the line $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+3x=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3x)^{2}-x=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-3 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,{u}^{2}-3\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n![](images/26/2687f0cc14eb0b476ea5f4ba7272e7f248bb7dcb276a0e2b0c39e551501b011d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(1-({u}^{2}-3))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea71a81-55b2-5821-a85d-2c2b860ef761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411729} {:answer/id #uuid "68da978e-5392-5c13-80d0-e709f72c6da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411734} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0953bd-cd3e-5daf-9baa-978871a2e773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411737} {:answer/id #uuid "98fb62ce-1156-5b60-96a5-36d7a22956c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411759} {:answer/id #uuid "a31dfc4c-cfac-5c12-8881-aa0d90cb5c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68da978e-5392-5c13-80d0-e709f72c6da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e959dbb-ec9c-5316-8bef-b8459b6fb499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411827}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fec53f8-0962-53b9-8a63-104c832ed68f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160710" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines $x+y=1,$ $x-y=1$ and the curve ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x-y=u$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+y=v$ and $x-y=u$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,(x-y)(x+y)=2,\\,⟹\\,uv=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}uv=2 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=2 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}uv=2 \\\\ u=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=1 \\\\ v=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{2}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c151e7fc8d150b9c8def69d609446b77424c2703decd9c8c55866cff2ae7437.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(2)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[v]_{1}^{2/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Area by Transforming to a Type I Region"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016 :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303 :topic/math-academy-id 1038 :topic/title "Integration Using the Double-Angle Formulas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7aa44b-cefc-5820-8f30-d5e2344ef950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321243} {:answer/id #uuid "296bd36f-2f4a-5195-8d23-67afa8466ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321469} {:answer/id #uuid "5994704b-000c-507d-a814-14894c25981b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321854} {:answer/id #uuid "663ac189-5c11-5931-88ff-1f3a097d9de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321939} {:answer/id #uuid "eacb236a-ca73-5bff-8d2f-ea5761a80a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eacb236a-ca73-5bff-8d2f-ea5761a80a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322985} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3bb291-72e9-573f-8060-c17a7f541fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323359}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fcddcef-1856-5848-a572-712ca0d53c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-38031" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acba3f29-0158-52f0-9456-91c5be61af4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127268} {:answer/id #uuid "b65a27eb-39be-5bb5-a399-1d44b7927674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127269} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc8225-ad29-548c-99c4-034d05c1b2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127270} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a0d310-0000-5652-8ada-f073e31a559d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127271} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc4c481-03e9-5e2b-b660-a09ea114f9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127272}] :answer-field/id #uuid "74727eb8-963c-5008-8d8c-d65c40ef415d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127267}] :question/id #uuid "4de8b5f4-2e7e-535e-81ad-97991889d6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-38127" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{π/3}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\sin θdrdθ$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78120fd-44f8-51c1-b9dd-5784c7ddde90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276989} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf96883-ff35-5cf9-b8c9-ef1449820eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277149} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b7cf35-e4e8-5645-988c-d9decc7eb447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277218} {:answer/id #uuid "d256a689-9115-5115-b8cc-dca87fe713d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277332} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b78b6c-d3db-508c-85cc-e0ca239ccf20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b7cf35-e4e8-5645-988c-d9decc7eb447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277218} :answer-field/id #uuid "2939712c-1519-5b89-99ce-fbfcd86f2f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aff73867-9b1f-5562-9960-420de4061c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-161049" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{sin}^{3}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\frac{1}{3}{sin}^{3}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59029508-b3c4-5856-9e82-8be13d20dfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321849} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe432d-69ae-5f45-8501-b2941dd6da47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321875} {:answer/id #uuid "73b5e790-d197-591e-8023-1b42b9c19647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322050} {:answer/id #uuid "84f4df8e-914d-5611-8d98-3e64db0593e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322199} {:answer/id #uuid "a8213e0d-3524-56f9-9f0b-d587a6a113bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe432d-69ae-5f45-8501-b2941dd6da47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321875} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8f76a21-833b-5523-ac04-4ad00b86b928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323497}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7818e95-0cd3-5ca9-8225-4d2224300f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38133" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17565734-926b-52e3-8121-3fbdaea7f5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348644} {:answer/id #uuid "4509f43f-e1e1-5642-b552-7ac3e8a361a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349073} {:answer/id #uuid "5b19110d-ad5e-5ffa-9e28-33a3151ac8f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349285} {:answer/id #uuid "670fa60a-c544-5a09-9fe7-cd3fdc72ce28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349391} {:answer/id #uuid "908f282c-0775-5149-a41c-32c4e538f86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b19110d-ad5e-5ffa-9e28-33a3151ac8f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349285} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f1e2bf0-e7fe-5f40-96b3-f24b49a5f589" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350865}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06a556db-bf08-5756-8218-a6278b5b6e16" :question/math-academy-id "q-83511" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =20[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10{[{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =10(1-0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "766cc68a-3d23-5f16-ba87-3f8f5800b901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352609} {:answer/id #uuid "77d5fbd2-7c92-549f-93b3-1fdc63f38113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352616} {:answer/id #uuid "a2097643-cd6a-574e-9d94-468a5f04b054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352954} {:answer/id #uuid "a485bba6-e236-5258-93a5-a3bb1a8603c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352972} {:answer/id #uuid "ef83d9b5-3baa-586b-9c44-1971f5324b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef83d9b5-3baa-586b-9c44-1971f5324b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353652} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe41175-b766-5595-800a-8ed923d6727b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354166}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1bcf047-04fc-54ad-9345-94ccc9c748a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83957" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =6[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /6}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431388eb-9598-575f-ad76-d3f33ee209fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352183} {:answer/id #uuid "44f3a20a-620f-5a8c-955b-ae48e7b8cd59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352204} {:answer/id #uuid "ae068552-f076-510a-8dee-4a77718965b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353042} {:answer/id #uuid "bd34ba33-c031-5097-a731-809dc1c41e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353178} {:answer/id #uuid "cc455a3f-3a88-5672-92b6-a2ec611e8f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd34ba33-c031-5097-a731-809dc1c41e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353178} :answer-field/id #uuid "43682371-f791-5e91-a473-d5d5a1fad81e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353933}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38812bca-4749-5693-ba84-2da96d03ab77" :question/math-academy-id "q-83958" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]_{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({1}^{2}-(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8-\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430733} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7ef1ee-27c1-50f3-af3d-a2b63e2d03d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430738} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3e76f1-42b1-5f3d-8b32-cf741baa2a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430740} {:answer/id #uuid "5e28ab03-180e-5b20-af37-557e7db4d91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430743} {:answer/id #uuid "77346f94-7bc7-5551-a9ff-5850737d6c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430733} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca73348-922c-58d8-8087-d4874ab8f01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430765}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c4c9c61-2471-5bbd-bac4-3d2dcc4d6268" :question/math-academy-id "q-83514" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{2}]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({2}^{2}-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =[4\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-(\\pi +1) \\\\  & =\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf5461b-da0e-570c-b9aa-467a2f82071d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430741} {:answer/id #uuid "5d101e08-dd8a-5f3e-96a5-138bde512f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430742} {:answer/id #uuid "681f0aba-b493-51e6-a417-42799a6f3a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430744} {:answer/id #uuid "73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430748} {:answer/id #uuid "e5117ec4-182c-58a0-848d-ff0ac8238d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430748} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4db397-2205-5620-8d4c-677784accd64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430764}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "414bd17b-1eab-5df7-bb84-eb1080abae7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161048" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(-1-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40fcf753-0267-53ac-aa07-33aca198c3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430737} {:answer/id #uuid "566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430739} {:answer/id #uuid "686056c3-1712-5837-8d5c-57ad9de19f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430745} {:answer/id #uuid "a84ffeca-f3ff-5817-85cf-b80c75007d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430752} {:answer/id #uuid "e43ffd51-6f41-504e-a3ea-d2e5f89becd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a63565-3b3e-5f4e-ac3a-4fbb44e6e16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9231aa24-53fe-5011-80b7-3b4b62ca8c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-83966" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))-(-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)-(-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}]}
